     "type": "intro",
     "title": "If We Cure Aging, Who Benefits First?",
     "content": "An earlier lesson in this book surveyed society and longevity broadly, covering how extended healthy lifespans might reshape retirement, careers, and family structure. This lesson narrows the focus to a harder set of specific ethical dilemmas that a broad overview tends to skip past. Suppose, for the sake of argument, that some version of Sinclair's vision succeeds and effective, biology-altering longevity interventions become available within a few decades. That success would not distribute itself evenly or automatically, and it would force societies to make explicit choices they currently avoid by treating aging as an unavoidable, universal fact rather than a treatable condition. Three specific dilemmas deserve close attention. First, resource allocation: does adding potentially decades of additional healthy life to a growing number of people strain food, housing, energy, and environmental systems, and if so, how should that concern be weighed against the individual benefit? Second, generational turnover: institutions from academia to corporate boardrooms to national governments rely, whether we admit it or not, on older leaders eventually stepping aside, and radically extended healthy lifespans could freeze that turnover in ways that affect everyone, not just the people receiving treatment. Third, unequal access: expensive frontier medicine has historically reached wealthy patients first, and a longevity intervention could turn additional healthy decades of life into a luxury good, widening existing gaps in life expectancy that already correlate strongly with income. None of these dilemmas has a clean answer, and reasoning through them is the actual point of this lesson.",
