     "type": "example",
     "title": "Case Study: Planck's Principle and the Politics of Not Retiring",
     "content": "The physicist Max Planck made an observation, often paraphrased today as science advances one funeral at a time, that even well-supported new ideas in a field frequently do not win over the senior figures already established in that field. Instead, the new idea gains ground primarily as an older generation retires or passes away and is replaced by researchers trained after the new idea was already part of the standard picture. Historians and sociologists of science have since found real statistical support for a version of this pattern in some fields, where senior, tenured researchers do appear to weight new competing ideas less favorably than younger scholars just entering the field. Radical life extension raises an unsettling structural question: if senior figures in science, law, corporate leadership, and government retain their positions and their existing views for dramatically longer than a normal career span, does this same friction against new ideas and new leadership intensify across every institution that currently relies on retirement, term limits, or mortality itself to create turnover? Long-tenured judges serving on high courts for many decades, corporate founders retaining board control long after their formative ideas have aged, and political leadership that persists across an unusually extended career are all examples of institutions that already strain against generational renewal, even without any life-extension technology involved. Radical life extension would not create this tension from nothing, but critics argue it would remove one of the few remaining, reliably universal mechanisms, natural retirement through aging and death, that currently forces institutional renewal to occur at all.",
